   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><br><div dir=\"ltr\"><div style=\"text-align:left\">Hi to all,<br><br>I am trying to develop a latent variable that represent a process, but I worry about its stability. This is what I did:<br><br>The model. I fixed external observed variables mean (intercept) and variance. If I use fixed.x = TRUE sem() estimates them any way and I lose df. data is provided in an attached file.<br><br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">data </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> read</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">csv</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;data.csv&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br>mod1 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&#39;<br>LV =~ clay.ratio + clay.B<br>LV ~ dem + river + lstm + lstsd <br><br># variances external drivers are fixed<br>dem ~~ 3.886*dem<br>river ~~ 14.373*river<br>lstm ~~ 0.183*lstm<br>lstsd ~~ 0.066*lstsd<br><br># intercepts external drivers are fixed<br>dem ~ 6.020 * 1<br>river ~ 7.2659 * 1<br>lstm ~  23.778 * 1<br>lstsd ~ 7.719 * 1<br><br>dem ~~ river + lstsd + lstm<br>river ~~  lstm + lstsd<br>lstm ~~ lstsd<br>clay.B ~~ river<br>clay.ratio ~~ clay.ratio<br>&#39;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>fit1 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> sem</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">mod1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">fixed</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">x </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> FALSE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> meanstructure </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> T</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></div></code></div><br>Then I could predict LV scores usinf lavPredict(fit1, &quot;lv&quot;), but I wondered how dependant is this factor from the sample data, so I ran the model as many times as samples I have leaving one out and computing its mean using <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/d/msg/lavaan/cuGujHtnCwQ/3BXy4Q5uPcwJ\" rel=\"nofollow\" target=\"_blank\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/d/msg/lavaan/cuGujHtnCwQ/3BXy4Q5uPcwJ&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792429088214000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3eiZJAnVvGCZ0avV7ZwcEx\">this approach</a> <br><br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">thing</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">to</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">save </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> rep</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">NA</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> nrow</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(data</span><span style=\"color:#000\"></span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> <br></span><span style=\"color:#008\">for</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i </span><span style=\"color:#008\">in</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\">nrow</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  </span><span style=\"color:#008\">out</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> sem</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">mod1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[-</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,],</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">fixed</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">x </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> FALSE</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> meanstructure </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> T</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>  thing</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">to</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">save</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">i</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mean</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">lavPredict</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#008\">object</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">out</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">type </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;lv&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><br></div></code></div><br>I have 317 samples, so I got 317 LV means:<br><br><img src=\"https://groups.google.com/group/lavaan/attach/287c5706e822/Auto%20Generated%20Inline%20Image%201?part=0.2&amp;authuser=0\" alt=\"\"><br>This is the LV summary of one of the 317 models<br><code><span style=\"color:#000\">summary(lavPredict</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#008\">object</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">out</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">type </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;lv&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></code> <br>Min.   :-3.3726  <br> 1st Qu.:-2.1144  <br> Median :-1.8563  <br> Mean   :-1.8158  <br> 3rd Qu.:-1.5200  <br> Max.   :-0.3238<br><br><br>I would expect a LV means much more stable.<br><br>Could you give any reason for this?<br><br>Thanks,<br>Marcos<br><br></div></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
